891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050823 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050823 #edb/ref 1031 "Proving Congruence Statements" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050823 #edb/ref 1033 1357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050823 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "69883e68-2851-532b-8bb3-c8eceb1d668d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110395 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2615" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110395 #edb/ref 1071 "In the figure below, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AC}$ is the bisector of $\\angle DAB$ and $m\\angle DAB = 79^\\circ.$ What is the measure of $\\angle DAC?$\n\n![](images/8d/8d02999cf6ae7ec69fee2cdac99b8dc214c59f1ed50bbf85bec6bec01f2212ef.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110395 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9612e5aa-af83-5ea5-8bf2-302834e1926a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110395 #edb/ref 1074 "An angle bisector divides the angle into two congruent angles with equal measures, so $m\\angle BAC = m\\angle DAC .$\n\n![](images/6e/6e7c8ce2713441e633d7ecc3548c504d2522b4771eb6355217d6ab766a647b67.png)\n\nEach of these congruent angles has a measure that is half the measure of the larger bisected angle. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DAC & =\\frac{m∠DAB}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{79}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={39.5}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110397 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2621" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110397 #edb/ref 1071 "In the figure below, ![](images/64/6450e078b6f1dec407838cfd67a8ec79507bc9ffa00ab12fc167d6087dc8cfe4.svg) is the bisector of ![](images/f5/f5f6b946618b35ae916b01cb82e1a5e741f41993f303f02b5c119351d7452346.svg) and ![](images/cf/cf8d89fc393869edb95a2fdb8b75d24bb04f3de676fd73cdd9d8a19af092a00a.svg) What is the measure of ![](images/8d/8d4751af85bf1737162fafc15e01e261b992f958ee7be84e5b03a2c7dcfedfbf.svg)\n\n![](images/91/910939d11fc9c0acdb5fbb7e073b67c8fcbf91ea5a88e74851f0f5f900f5123a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110397 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec3e6d87-e38e-5aa6-b064-f3c15a51a836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110397 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the angles ![](images/f5/f5f6b946618b35ae916b01cb82e1a5e741f41993f303f02b5c119351d7452346.svg) and ![](images/d0/d0d040e66003036f8fa31da39da08d78506511f501b1a3f5cd1f39efe16f910c.svg) are supplementary, we have \n\n![](images/47/47f63dede9eaaa0f1ed64bc2cc6110bdbab7f3d52052201284417d1bf7336e5a.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/64/6450e078b6f1dec407838cfd67a8ec79507bc9ffa00ab12fc167d6087dc8cfe4.svg) is the bisector of ![](images/f5/f5f6b946618b35ae916b01cb82e1a5e741f41993f303f02b5c119351d7452346.svg), we obtain\n\n![](images/4b/4b9eaaf39d74d934992b68c439bff0bed5c7ef5fa1f0d59620993caed61f0860.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110398 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110398 #edb/ref 1071 "In the figure below, $\\overrightarrow{OC}$ is the bisector of $\\angle AOB.$ If $m \\angle AOD = 90^\\circ$ and $m \\angle COD = 35^\\circ,$ find the measure of $\\angle BOD.$\n\n![](images/0b/0b76c6ee6e576fa2701993e941c27f1168c213d9f5f26b1aa8a50759c3711404.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110398 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4304d42-1b4c-5ade-86d2-8ed44a952fc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110398 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/5a/5ae4873418247e8d825233f903ed359ebee4d2c51ad73e6c7763102a23acded6.png)\n\nBy the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC & =m∠AOD-m∠COD \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}-{35}^{∘} \\\\  & ={55}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm \\angle AOB =2 m \\angle AOC = 110^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFinally, the angle addition postulate gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & =m∠AOB-m∠AOD \\\\  & ={110}^{∘}-{90}^{∘} \\\\  & ={20}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110399 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2628" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110399 #edb/ref 1071 "In the figure below, $\\angle BAD$ is a straight angle, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$ and $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AF}$ is the bisector of $\\angle BAC .$ What is the measure of $\\angle GAF?$\n\n![](images/45/458893f38204421326fd7dcb0693b1688eeb61fb3988e00b2d36763b47adc2d2.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110399 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b3dbb627-0262-5fdd-ae6b-ba669e4fac6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110399 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/3b/3b0fddd3229947725b0f4f07e58ce9bfac3a9e7e348c25757162f5bc8e03d699.png)\n\nLet $m \\angle FAC=x$ and $m \\angle CAG=y.$ Then,\n\n$$\nm \\angle GAF=m\\angle FAC + m \\angle CAG=x+y.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AF}$ is the bisector of $\\angle BAC$, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle BAC=2x.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle CAD=2y.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\angle BAD$ is a straight angle, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BAC+m∠CAD & =m∠BAD \\\\ 2x+2y & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x+y & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $m \\angle GAF=x+y=90^\\circ.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110766 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110766 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110766 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110766 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110766 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110766 #edb/ref 1039 "Using the Property of a Bisection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110766 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110766 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d3162bc9-a06e-50d9-aaab-628438daa9fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110767 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110767 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110767 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110767 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110767 #edb