#edb/ref 17592186507899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508008 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508008 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508008 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508008 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508008 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a5434d2b-7760-524c-b9cd-5f9df1cefcab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508021 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508021 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508021 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508021 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d0bd8a51-6747-5bfc-b459-183d1248b264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508042 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508042 #edb/ref 1069 "q-117143" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508042 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508042 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/62/6239a47a2bd88b631f9058c7afe3b59c10b8e71fb8957fe7a354cc7e72daf69d.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $m∠BOC={72}^{∘}$ and $m∠AOC=7x+{9}^{∘}.$ If $\\overset{\\to }{OC}$ is the bisector of $∠AOB,$ find the value of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508042 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b7d3007-c733-562f-b061-3d6372ea77d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508042 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/6a/6a49c1d69a34d0159cd24869b51cb7a517021a147e0e831b969c1135f6b533fc.png)\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Thus, $∠AOC≅∠BOC.$ Therefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC & =m∠BOC \\\\ 7x+{9}^{∘} & ={72}^{∘} \\\\ 7x & ={63}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={9}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508067 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508067 #edb/ref 1069 "q-117146" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508067 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508067 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8f/8f818ebe4aea90c33ba1eb3feccf988b0486565da9177df1e98460a950f8b22e.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overset{\\to }{OC}$ is the bisector of $∠AOB.$ If $m∠AOB={58}^{∘}$, find $m∠AOC.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508067 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66ea6fa9-fa1e-5e19-8e8b-006a3addc0db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508067 #edb/ref 1074 "The bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Each of these congruent angles has a measure that is half the measure of the larger bisected angle. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC & =\\frac{m∠AOB}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{58}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={29}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508081 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508081 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508081 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508081 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7b/7ba1daa74999732ba112150ad8ecb0958f80057a4aeb8ff52c96bf9b44a8337c.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ is the bisector of $∠AOC.$ If $m∠BOA={131}^{∘}$ and $m∠BOC={73}^{∘},$ find $m∠DOA.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508081 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c39734e-60e8-53eb-a61a-e2f6712a7e91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508081 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/68/68c84f70ff4fcd384c21414958ea696469ac6c64749d31c7db7f8792599d77ce.png)\n\nBy the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA & =m∠BOA-m∠BOC \\\\  & ={131}^{∘}-{73}^{∘} \\\\  & ={58}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DOA & =\\frac{m∠COA}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{58}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={29}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508085 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508085 #edb/ref 1069 "q-173763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508085 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508085 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f0/f0662ae0e6957786ab7f3821a932e1f36a1c8ca6e8299709daa5e323601194fa.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠BOA$ is a straight angle, and $\\overset{\\to }{OC}$ is the bisector of $∠BOD.$ If $m∠DOA={84}^{∘},$ then $m∠COA=${{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508085 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a317f88-19a8-56a2-bc2b-47dd254aaf3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508085 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/52/5291d267ce1e895cfe75261a36c9dbc660a32935b6858ec8986379ee8c8bb766.png)\n\nSince the angles $∠BOD$ and $∠DOA$ are supplementary, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & ={180}^{∘}-m∠DOA \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{84}^{∘} \\\\  & ={96}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COD & =\\frac{m∠BOD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{96}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={48}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA & =m∠COD+m∠DOA \\\\  & ={48}^{∘}+{84}^{∘} \\\\  & ={132}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508092 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508092 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76644" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508092 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508092 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fb/fb5fddbf843a89ffbefeaed1976a2bfd159fea9a41665f99aee260f110016e1c.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{OE}$ are the bisectors of $∠AOC$ and $∠BOC,$ respectively. If $m∠DOC=x,$ $m∠COE=2x+{2}^{∘},$ and $m∠AOB={130}^{∘},$ find the value of $m∠BOC.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508092 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b176a2ae-2130-5324-9a8b-d961a2933f07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508092 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/48/484a70ad0c9eeca1ea03db65f5c687074189ac93f9894b64598278dc285d0d83.png)\n\nFirst, let's express the measures of $∠AOC$ and $∠BOC,$ in terms of $x.$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ is the bisector of $∠AOC,$ we have\n\n$$\nm∠AOC=2m∠DOC=2x.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\to }{OE}$ is the bisector of $∠BOC,$ we have\n\n$$\nm∠BOC=2m∠COE=4x+{4}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSince $m∠AOB={130}^{∘},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC+m∠BOC & =m∠AOB \\\\ 2x+4x+{4}^{∘} & ={130}^{∘} \\\\ 6x+{4}^{∘} & ={130}^{∘} \\\\ 6x & ={126}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={21}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are asked to find $m∠BOC.$ Substituting $x={21}^{∘}$ into the expression for $m∠BOC$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOC & =4({21}^{∘})+{4}^{∘} \\\\  & ={84}^{∘}+{4}^{∘} \\\\  & ={88}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508096 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508096 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76634" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508096 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508096 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/38/38f49977e7d2f280dec7b433e167b3e081ed10716715e7c9caa669e57fe0701a.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠BOA$ is a straight angle,  and $\\overset{\\to }{OC}$ is the bisector of $∠BOD.$ If $m∠DOA={134}^{∘},$ find $m∠COA.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508096 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c102a149-79c1-57ef-8166-900c7a1db3c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508096 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/8d/8dfbfa4aaa55e2f8175fe829fccc945d29733a84fc5c1641f099b09d805ada2b.png)\n\nSince the angles $∠BOD$ and $∠DOA$ are supplementary, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & ={180}^{∘}-m∠DOA \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{134}^{∘} \\\\  & ={46}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COD & =\\frac{m∠BOD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{46}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={23}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA & =m∠COD+m∠DOA \\\\  & ={23}^{∘}+{134}^{∘} \\\\  & ={157}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508099 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508099 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508099 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508099 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7b/7bd5be67491f109f8041d33768aca872f9dc7242dd1d91bcbfe116a8277f5bf1.png)\n\nIn the diagram above $∠BOA$ is a straight angle. Given that $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{OE}$ are the bisectors of $∠COA$ and $∠BOC$, respectively, find $m∠EOD.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508099 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cee4d62e-6294-5674-b439-787997c480d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508099 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/19/19334b594e8567464103bacc4f0978cbbbc664c3cc5a1a6edd5789e822030c4b.png)\n\nLet $m∠COD=x$ and $m∠EOC=y.$ Then,\n\n$$\nm∠EOD=m∠COD+m∠EOC=x+y.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{⟶}{OD}$ is a bisector of $∠COA,$ we have\n\n$$\nm∠COA=2x.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{⟶}{OE}$ is a bisector of $∠BOC,$ we have\n\n$$\nm∠BOC=2y.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $∠AOB$ is a straight angle, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA+m∠BOC & =m∠AOB \\\\ 2x+2y & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x+y & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $m∠EOD=x+y={90}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508111 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508111 #edb/ref 1069 "q-117261" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508111 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508111 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/23/23794678967f5b2cd1d409f116a9083aa60f942737ad41fa960e91263691f8c7.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ is the bisector of $∠COA.$ If $m∠BOA={127}^{∘}$ and $m∠BOD={75}^{∘},$ find $m∠BOC.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508111 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e88bb8a0-0e79-51e0-a5d2-1fbecece5fbd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508111 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/72/72c0047a8d0b513a5159d31addf0e4c0c2eeefe0e73ceeadf8e154c37f583039.png)\n\nBy the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DOA & =m∠BOA-m∠BOD \\\\  & ={127}^{∘}-{75}^{∘} \\\\  & ={52}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠DOA=m∠COD={52}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOC & =m∠BOD-m∠COD \\\\  & ={75}^{∘}-{52}^{∘} \\\\  & ={23}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
